-- | rustfmt's `fn_call_width` default.
-- |
-- | A call whose **argument list** — everything inside the parentheses — is
-- | wider than this is written one argument per line. Measured on the
-- | arguments rather than on the line, like `CHAIN_WIDTH` and unlike
-- | `max_width`, which is what makes it usable in an emitter that does not
-- | know what column it is at.
-- |
-- | Confirmed at the boundary: `"{}", describe(&E::Missing(String::from("x")))`
-- | is 61 characters and is spread, and the same call one character shorter is
-- | not.
-- |
-- | **Applied to `println!` and not yet to an ordinary call.** A long
-- | `f(a, b)` is still emitted on one line and rustfmt will break it, which is
-- | a stated gap rather than an oversight: `print` is the only call the
-- | example corpus generates long enough to hit it, so it is the only one
-- | whose behaviour is currently measured. Doc 18 §11.5 records it.
export(crate) const FN_CALL_WIDTH : usize = 60
